/** @page conventions Conventions

Coding conventions used by this library.

## Pointers

This library mostly assumes that pointers are not `NULL`. Functions will not
return `NULL` if not documented otherwise and passing `NULL` as arguments is
undefined if not documented otherwise.

Function parameters that accept `NULL` have their names prefixed with `optional_`.
Non-null return values and arguments are attributed with @ref GP_NONNULL_ARGS,
@ref GP_NONNULL_RETURN, and @ref GP_NONNULL_ARGS_AND_RETURN attributes when
applicable for some enforcement from the compiler, although the attributes will
be omitted from the HTML documentation.

## Const Correctness

Raw pointers that are not supposed to mutate the data are appropriately marked
as `const` for the most part. However, we have a few typedefs for some pointer
types like @ref GPString. We generally avoid typedeffing pointers away, because
it prevents using `const` and often decreases readability, but it is appropriate
for some abstractions when the "pointerness" is not important. We might provide
additional `const` typedefs like @ref GPStringConst when appropriate.

## Naming Conventions

Understanding our naming conventions can help with navigating the library and
memorizing important symbols. Here we only document the conventions that are
relevant for users. Contributors should just try to follow the conventions of
the surrounding code when modifying internals.

### Namespacing

All symbols are prefixed with `gp_`, `GP`, or `GP_` depending on casing (see
below). Functions and macros may be additionally prefixed with an abbreviated
class name or module name. For example, functions operating on @ref GPString
belong to @ref GPString class, so they are prefixed with `gp_str_`, and functions
that allocate memory belong to the memory module, so they are prefixed with
`gp_mem_`. However, utility functions like @ref gp_next_power_of_two() do not
belong to any class/module, so the class/module prefix is omitted.

The only exception to these rules is `GPC_IMPLEMENTATION`, which is defined by
the user when compiling the single header library. This uses `GPC_` prefix
instead of `GP_` prefix.

All public header files are prefixed with `gp`. Most importantly this fixes
conflicts between standard `string.h` and our `gpstring.h`.

### Macros and Enumeration Constants

Macros and enumeration constants are `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE`. Exception to this
are public macro functions that behave like regular functions like @ref gp_println(),
these use `snake_case` instead to indicate that they don't evaluate their
arguments multiple times during runtime and that they will evaluate to an
expression, not a statement like `do {...} while(0)`.

### Structures and Unions

Typedefs to compound types or opaque types use PascalCase. Struct tags match
the typedef alias to enable forward declaration and for C++ compatibility.

### Fundamental Types

Typedefs to fundamental types and enumeration types use snake_case with `_t`
suffix to indicate that implicit integer conversions may occur and that
arithmetic operators can be used. Exception to this are opaque types like
@ref GPThreadKey, which is almost always implemented as an integer, but this is
not guaranteed and the type is anyway not supposed to be used like a regular
integer and should be treated as opaque.

One might point out that POSIX reserves `_t` suffixes. We justify our usage of
`_t` by pointing out that not only `_t` is idiomatic in C (possibly due to
ignorance of POSIX reserved names, but this makes no difference), but also any
potential naming collisions are avoided with the `gp_` prefix.
*/
